    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In the last 60 years, humankind has littered the space around our planet with\r\ndebris. The accumulation of that debris presents dangers to both current and future\r\nactivities in space. In response to this problem, policymakers at the national and\r\ninternational levels have enforced policies to mitigate the creation of new debris.\r\nThis paper examines the relationship between the growth of the orbital debris\r\nproblem and those policies, evaluating potential political science explanations to\r\nexplain the relationship. The results show that Kingdon’s “three streams” theory,\r\nwhere the convergence of a problem, a policy solution, and a political opportunity\r\npropels an issue onto the political agenda, best accounts for the positive\r\nrelationship observed between orbital debris and policy responses. Finally, this\r\npaper evaluates how the entry of new private, players into the launch game will\r\nworsen the orbital debris problem long-term. It examines the relationship between\r\nthe debris problem and policy into the future, concluding that the continuance of\r\ncurrent mitigation efforts is a more likely policy future than a shift to the type of\r\nactive removal policies necessary to solve the orbital debris problem.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Wilson, K. (2022). The Sky is Falling: An Evaluation of Policy Responses to the Orbital Debris Problem. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/jx926rq3839",
            "type": "preferred citation",
